Ciguapa is the second planet from the star Aurelius, orbiting between the sun-scorched [[Tartessos]] and the volcanic [[Mictlán]]. It is a massive terrestrial planet, slightly larger and more massive than [[Adonia]], and is shrouded entirely in a thick, opaque atmosphere of reflective clouds. To the naked eye, Ciguapa appears as the brightest object in the Adonian dawn and dusk sky, outshining even the gas giant [[Quetzal]] due to its high albedo.

Classified as a super-greenhouse world, Ciguapa represents a planetary environment taken to the extreme. While it resides within the optimistic edge of the habitable zone, its runaway greenhouse effect has resulted in surface temperatures exceeding 735 K, hotter even than the dayside of Tartessos. The planet is often cited in Adonian comparative planetology as a cautionary example of climate feedback loops.

Ciguapa possesses a retrograde rotation, spinning in the opposite direction to its orbit and the majority of other planets in the system. This rotation is incredibly slow; a single sidereal day on Ciguapa lasts longer than its entire year. This unique characteristic is believed to be the result of a massive impact event during the system's formation or strong atmospheric tidal torques exerted by Aurelius.

==Physical characteristics==
===Atmosphere===
The atmosphere of Ciguapa is its most defining feature, composed primarily of carbon dioxide with thick cloud layers consisting of sulfuric acid droplets. These clouds form a global deck that completely obscures the surface from visual observation. The atmosphere is so dense that the surface pressure is approximately 93 times that of Adonia, equivalent to the pressure found 900 meters underwater on Earth-like worlds.

The upper atmosphere exhibits a phenomenon known as super-rotation. While the planet itself rotates very slowly, the atmosphere whips around the planet at speeds up to 360 km/h, circling the globe in just four Adonian days. This rapid circulation distributes heat efficiently, ensuring that the nightside is nearly as hot as the dayside, unlike the extreme temperature variance seen on [[Tartessos]].

===Surface geology===
Radar mapping conducted by orbital probes has revealed a surface dominated by vast volcanic plains and gently rolling uplands. Unlike [[Adonia]], Ciguapa lacks plate tectonics to recycle its crust. Instead, the planet undergoes periodic resurfacing events where massive localized volcanism floods large portions of the surface with fresh lava. The surface rock is kept plastic and semi-viscous by the immense heat and pressure.

There are few impact craters on Ciguapa, indicating that the surface is geologically young, likely less than 500 million years old. The dense atmosphere filters out smaller meteors, which burn up before impact, leaving only the largest impactors to scar the surface.

==Orbit and rotation==
Ciguapa orbits Aurelius at an average distance of 0.45 AU, completing one revolution every 110.0 standard days. Its orbit is the most circular of all the planets, with an eccentricity of less than 0.01.

The planet rotates in a retrograde direction (east to west) once every 243.02 days. Because its rotation period is longer than its orbital period, a solar day on Ciguapa (sunrise to sunrise) takes approximately 117 Adonian days. The sun rises in the west and sets in the east.

==Name and etymology==
The name Ciguapa is derived from Insulonian folklore, referring to a mythical creature described as a woman with backward-facing feet who dwells in the high mountains and deep forests. This name was chosen by early astronomers who noted the planet's peculiar retrograde rotation—it moves "backwards" compared to the other terrestrial worlds like [[Adonia]] and [[Aztlán]]. The opaque clouds were also seen as a metaphor for the creature's elusive nature.

In Sanese astronomical catalogs, the planet is designated Kirigoromosei (霧衣星), translating to the Mist-Veiled Star. This name references the impenetrable cloud layer that permanently hides the planet's surface, likening it to a figure wearing a robe of mist.

